## **The One Finding That Is Strengthened, Not Weakened**

There is a finding in the prior analysis that these amendments actually strengthen rather than complicate. The prior report noted that Dan's deepest failure was 'that he knew — at every stage — that something was wrong, and he chose the hope of what they had been over the evidence of what they were becoming.' The financial history adds texture to what 'what they had been' meant to Dan. He moved Annie to New York. He funded years of their shared life. He built something real, with real resources, across a real period of time. His inability to leave was not only the product of a trauma bond or an addiction to hope. It was the product of a person who had invested a decade of his life — financially, emotionally, and structurally — into a shared future that he was watching dissolve. The prior report identified his attachment correctly. These amendments explain it more fully.

**FINAL SUMMARY OF AMENDMENTS: Three contextual corrections accepted. The financial narrative of the prior report is revised to reflect a decade of cyclical financial roles rather than a structurally Annie-supported dynamic. Dan's involuntary job loss is confirmed and removes the implication of chosen unemployment. The 2023 debt period establishes a prior financial inversion that makes Annie's 2024 grievances historically partial rather than structurally definitive. None of these amendments alter the terminal phase analysis, the comparative culpability finding, or the behavioral evidence base on which all prior conclusions rest.**

